The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
putname(name);
if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
    lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
    goto retry;
}
```
Reason:
The premature call to `putname(name);` releases the `name` object before the function checks if it needs to retry the operation. If a retry is necessary, the function attempts to access the already freed `name` object, leading to a use-after-free error.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// Moved putname(name); to after the retry check
if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
    lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
    goto retry;
}
putname(name);
```
Reason:
By moving `putname(name);` to after the retry check, the `name` object remains valid during the retry process. This ensures that the function does not attempt to access a freed object, thus preventing the use-after-free error.

